{"name":"Physalis Peal","hex":"#e1d8bb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"physalis","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["bladder"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the genus Physalis.","Fruit of such a plant, a yellow-orange berry, typically firm in texture with a mild, refreshing acidity."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/physalis"},{"term":"peal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A loud sound, or a succession of loud sounds, as of bells, thunder, cannon, shouts, laughter, of a multitude, etc.","A set of bells tuned to each other according to the diatonic scale."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To sound with a peal or peals.","To utter or sound loudly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peal"}],"entries":[{"title":"Physalis","blocks":[{"text":"Physalis is a genus of approximately 75 to 90 flowering plants in the nightshade family (Solanaceae), which are native to the Americas and Australasia. At least 46 species are endemic to Mexico. Cultivated and weedy species have been introduced worldwide. A defining feature of Physalis is a large, papery husk derived from the calyx, which partly or fully encloses the fruit. Many species bear edible fruit, and some species are cultivated."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Starr_061225-2955_Physalis_peruviana.jpg/330px-Starr_061225-2955_Physalis_peruviana.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physalis","wikidata":"Q158663"},{"title":"Peal","blocks":[{"text":"In campanology, a peal is the special name given to a specific type of performance of change ringing which meets certain exacting conditions for duration, complexity and quality."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Penkridge_peal_board_first_peal_1832.jpg/330px-Penkridge_peal_board_first_peal_1832.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&ut